```

for shadcn-compatible semantic styling,

or:

```tsx
className="bg-neutral-950 text-neutral-100 border-neutral-700"
```

for explicit Vouch styling.

Use semantic tokens for shadcn primitives. Use explicit Tailwind colors for branded custom surfaces. There. The CSS goblin has been returned to containment.

Yes — for **fonts**, your `layout.tsx` approach is basically the official Next way: import from `next/font/google`, configure once, put the font variables on `<html>`, and use those variables from Tailwind/classes. Next says `next/font` self-hosts/optimizes fonts, removes external requests, and can be applied globally from the root layout; CSS-variable usage is the Tailwind-friendly path. citeturn624502search0turn624502search3

Your `layout.tsx` is fine. Tiny nit goblin: your OG/Twitter image paths should be `"/VOUCHdark.png"`, not `"public/VOUCHdark.png"` if the asset is in `/public`.

For **Tailwind v4 colors**, your `globals.css` is slightly wrong in naming. Tailwind v4 theme variables that generate utilities need namespaces like `--color-background`, `--color-primary`, `--font-brand`, etc. The docs say `@theme` variables create utility classes; plain `--background` alone is just a CSS variable, not automatically `bg-background`. citeturn673536search1

So this:

```css
@theme {
  --background: #0a0a0a;
}
```

should be more like:

```css
@theme {
  --color-background: #0a0a0a;
  --color-foreground: #f5f5f5;
  --color-primary: #2563eb;
  --color-border: #404040;

  --font-sans: var(--font-sans);
  --font-brand: var(--font-brand);
  --font-display: var(--font-display);
  --font-mono: var(--font-mono);
}
```

But because your `next/font` variables are also named `--font-sans`, don’t reuse the exact same name inside `@theme` pointing to itself. That’s the CSS snake eating its own tail, very frontend, very stupid.

Better:

In `layout.tsx`, keep:

```tsx
variable: "--font-archivo"
variable: "--font-archivo-black"
variable: "--font-bebas"
variable: "--font-jetbrains"
```

Then in `globals.css`:

```css
@theme {
  --font-sans: var(--font-archivo);
  --font-brand: var(--font-archivo-black);
  --font-display: var(--font-bebas);
  --font-mono: var(--font-jetbrains);
}
```

Your `components.json` says shadcn is configured with CSS variables enabled and points to `app/globals.css`, so this setup matches the shadcn model too. fileciteturn11file2L8-L15

Your `tsconfig` is strict and modern, including `strict`, `noUncheckedIndexedAccess`, and `exactOptionalPropertyTypes`, which explains why TypeScript keeps acting like a tiny courthouse clerk with a clipboard. fileciteturn11file0L6-L12

Verdict: **layout font strategy good; globals token naming needs cleanup.** Your current `globals.css` has the right idea, but Tailwind v4 wants `--color-*` and `--font-*` theme variables, not naked `--background` pretending it owns a utility empire.

