Yes — that specific second radial is the culprit.

```css
radial-gradient(circle at 80% 0%, oklch(13.629% 0.02215 248.62 / 0.24), transparent 24rem)
```

It is not green by hue in the obvious sense; hue `248.62` is blue/cyan territory. But because it is **very dark**, **low chroma**, and alpha-blended over near-black, it can visually read as a muddy green/teal cast. In OKLCH, the third channel is hue and the second is chroma, so even small chroma values can tint a dark background when layered in gradients. MDN describes OKLCH as lightness/chroma/hue coordinates, with alpha supported after the slash. citeturn233048search0

I’d remove that radial entirely or make it pure neutral. The grid lines are fine; the tinted top-right radial is what gives the page that swampy cast.

